{"name":"White Gauze","hex":"#c9c2bd","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"},{"term":"gauze","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A thin fabric with a loose, open weave.","A similar bleached cotton fabric used as a surgical dressing."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To apply a dressing of gauze; to cover with gauze.","To mist; to make or become gauze-like."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gauze"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gauze","blocks":[{"text":"Gauze is a thin, translucent fabric with a loose open weave. In technical terms, \"gauze\" is a weave structure in which the weft yarns are arranged in pairs and are crossed before and after each warp yarn, keeping the weft firmly in place. This weave structure is used to add stability to the fabric, which is important when using fine yarns loosely spaced. However, this weave structure can be used with any weight of yarn, and can be seen in some rustic textiles made from coarse hand-spun plant fiber yarns. Gauze is widely used for medical dressings."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Sad_Arcadia_Child_Under_Veil.jpg/330px-Sad_Arcadia_Child_Under_Veil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gauze","wikidata":"Q618710"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/MaksimovaDressGiselle.jpg/500px-MaksimovaDressGiselle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/MaksimovaDressGiselle.jpg/1280px-MaksimovaDressGiselle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/MaksimovaDressGiselle.jpg/500px-MaksimovaDressGiselle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tutu ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMaksimovaDressGiselle.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Zestientje.jpg/500px-Zestientje.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Zestientje.jpg/1280px-Zestientje.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Zestientje.jpg/500px-Zestientje.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gauze swab","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZestientje.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Boule_de_gaze_01.jpg/500px-Boule_de_gaze_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"h